# Copy to .env — .env is gitignored.

# ─── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Postgres 16 with pgvector, tsvector and pg_trgm available.
# Locally:  docker run --rm -d -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=standfast \
#             pgvector/pgvector:pg16
#
# DATABASE_URL is read by two consumers that want opposite privileges:
#
#   the API          — must NOT bypass row-level security
#   everything else  — the integration suite, its harness and db:down, which
#                      create roles and read across orgs, so they need the owner
#
# The owner is the default below, because that is what every command in the
# README except the last one needs. Swap in the restricted line before
# `pnpm start`: the API reads pg_roles at start-up and refuses to serve on a
# superuser or BYPASSRLS connection, because every tenant policy would be
# silently inactive while the application looked perfectly healthy. The refusal
# prints the two statements that create the role.
#
# Setting this also switches on the *.integration.test.ts suite, which expects
# a throwaway database it may create roles in. Do not point it at anything you
# care about.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:standfast@localhost:5432/standfast
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://standfast_api:standfast_api@localhost:5432/standfast   # pnpm start

# The owner connection. Migrations and bootstrap prefer it over DATABASE_URL,
# and the README's role-creation step runs `psql "$DIRECT_URL"` — so keep it
# set locally even though the string is identical to the one above.
DIRECT_URL=postgres://postgres:standfast@localhost:5432/standfast

# On Neon the two differ for a second reason. The pooled endpoint (-pooler)
# multiplexes connections and does not hold the session state some DDL and
# advisory locks need, so migrations run against the direct URL. Getting this
# wrong fails only in production, and confusingly.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://…@ep-xxx-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/standfast   # app
# DIRECT_URL=postgres://…@ep-xxx.region.aws.neon.tech/standfast            # migrations

# ─── API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=8080
NODE_ENV=development

# ─── Notes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No model provider key belongs here yet, because nothing in the tree makes a
# model call: phase 0 shipped no intelligence and phase 1, extraction, has not
# started. The first key to land will be for extraction and embeddings — see
# docs/06-retrieval.md for what the read path is and is not allowed to call.
#
# SUPERMEMORY_CC_API_KEY does not belong here either. It is a Claude Code
# plugin credential read from the developer's shell, and a key in the project
# tree would imply a runtime dependency that does not exist (CLAUDE.md).

# ─── Extraction (phase 1) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Which extractor, embedder and dedup judge this deployment uses. Both the API
# and the worker read these: the API stamps the derived extractor_version into
# every job payload, and the worker claims section_extractions with it, so a
# disagreement would extract at a version nobody asked for. The worker refuses
# to start when its ports do not match what is configured here.
#
# The defaults name phase 1c's deterministic fakes and are left commented out
# on purpose. They are not a fallback to something real — docs/05-extraction.md
# requires that no default reaches the network without being asked to, and a
# fake that cannot reach anything is the strongest form of that. Phase 1d adds
# real adapters, and with them the outbound allowlist that decides which
# destinations may be named here at all.
#
# STANDFAST_EXTRACTION_PROMPT_VERSION=none
# STANDFAST_EXTRACTION_MODEL=fake-bullet-1
# STANDFAST_EMBEDDING_MODEL=fake-bow-1
# STANDFAST_JUDGE_MODEL=fake-judge-1

# ─── Egress (docs/13-threat-model.md) ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The outbound counterpart to WIRE_ALLOWLIST. One entry per line or per `;`,
# three fields: purpose, URL, pinned model version.
#
#   STANDFAST_EGRESS_ALLOWLIST="
#     extraction https://api.example.com  some-model-2026-01-01
#     embedding  https://embed.example.com some-embedder-2026-01-01
#   "
#
# A destination not on the list is not called, and the refusal is at start-up
# rather than at call time — a check on the hot path fails once traffic is
# already flowing. Rules, and why:
#
#   1. A pinned version, never a floating alias. `…-latest` is refused: an
#      alias makes an entry stop describing what it admitted without the entry
#      changing (docs/02-principles.md, 12).
#   2. `reranking` may not be configured yet. Extraction, embedding and the
#      dedup judge send stored prose in a batch job; reranking sends a task
#      description a developer typed minutes ago, on the read path, in real
#      time. That is a second procurement decision with its own consent, and it
#      must not be switchable on by editing a phase-1 deployment's config.
#   3. http is admitted only to a host the operator runs — loopback, a private
#      range, or a bare container name. Self-hosting must work; plaintext to
#      somebody else's server must not.
#
# Leave it unset and this deployment calls nothing, which is correct for phase
# 1c: its extractor, embedder and judge are deterministic fakes and reach no
# network at all. The worker refuses to start with entries it would never use,
# because permission granted and not exercised outlives the review that granted
# it.
# STANDFAST_EGRESS_ALLOWLIST=

# Provider credentials, read only by the worker, and only when the egress
# allowlist above names a destination that needs them. A worker configured for
# a provider without its key refuses to start rather than failing on the first
# job.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# VOYAGE_API_KEY=
